    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d684">

                <pb facs="0799" n="687" sameAs="frus:frus1935v03/pg687"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">393.1111 Bush, Henry J./35: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Consul at Canton</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Fletcher</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Canton</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1935-06-21T17:00:00+08:00">June 21, 1935—5
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received June 21—3:15 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>Father Henry J. Bush officially delivered by Marshall Chen Chi Tang<anchor n="9"
                        xml:id="d684-f1"/> to this Consulate General 3 p.m. today. Bush in good
                    health notwithstanding 7 weeks’ captivity and upon his request was placed on
                    afternoon express train for Hong Kong accompanied by Father Charles P. Hilbert.
                    Bush ascribes his release entirely to the strenuous efforts of the three first
                    group army divisions mobilized by Marshal Chen Chi Tang for Bush’s rescue.
                    Repeated to the Department, the Legation, Swatow and to Nanking.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Fletcher</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d684-f1" n="9" xml:id="d684-f1-content">
                <p>Director of peace preservation at Canton.</p>
            </note>
